    "What is your relationship with mathematics?": "I’ve always found math fascinating but challenging. As a full-time student in upper secondary school, I use it for problem-solving in biology and philosophy. I enjoy its logic but sometimes struggle with abstract concepts, especially in areas like calculus. It’s a mix of curiosity and frustration.",
    "Do you ever get anxious when thinking about mathematics?": "I don’t get anxious about math itself but sometimes feel overwhelmed when tackling complex problems. My neuroticism score is moderate, so I might worry about not understanding something quickly. However, I use techniques like breaking problems into smaller steps to stay calm.",
    "Did you ever use AI to support your math learning in the last year? If yes, how was your experience?": "Yes, I used AI tools like Khan Academy and Wolfram Alpha last year. They helped me visualize equations and check my work. Initially, I was skeptical, but they made complex topics like algebraic equations more approachable. I’d recommend them for students who need extra practice.",
    "How would you explain, step by step, how to solve a second order algebraic equation?": "First, write the equation in standard form: ax² + bx + c = 0. Then, calculate the discriminant (b² - 4ac). If it’s positive, you get two real solutions; if zero, one real solution; if negative, two complex solutions. Finally, use the quadratic formula: x = [-b ± √(discriminant)] / (2a).",
    "How would you explain, step by step, how to find the stationary points of an equation y=f(x)?": "To find stationary points, take the first derivative f’(x) of the function. Set f’(x) = 0 and solve for x. These x-values are critical points. Then, plug them back into f(x) to find the corresponding y-values. Check if they’re maxima, minima, or inflection points using the second derivative.",
